1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,489
Zeus swallowed his own wife.

2
00:00:02,609 --> 00:00:04,089
She was pregnant.

3
00:00:04,439 --> 00:00:06,475
He did it on purpose.

4
00:00:06,595 --> 00:00:09,785
And an ancient Greek poem tells us why.

5
00:00:10,135 --> 00:00:13,900
This was after Zeus had won a ten-year war.

6
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:17,961
After he had thrown down a monster with a hundred heads.

7
00:00:18,081 --> 00:00:21,917
After the other gods had urged him to be their king.

8
00:00:22,317 --> 00:00:26,892
To see why, we have to go back to the first ruler of the sky.

9
00:00:27,012 --> 00:00:30,247
And to a warning he gave his own sons.

10
00:00:30,647 --> 00:00:34,760
On the way, we will find a stone that was fed to a god.

11
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:39,674
And a painter who drew the monster very differently from the poet.

12
00:00:40,274 --> 00:00:44,745
The poem is the Theogony, by a Greek poet named Hesiod.

13
00:00:44,945 --> 00:00:49,336
Most scholars date it to about seven hundred B.C.

14
00:00:49,736 --> 00:00:53,201
The story of the world begins with Chaos.

15
00:00:53,401 --> 00:00:55,468
Then Gaia, the Earth.

16
00:00:55,668 --> 00:01:04,113
And from Gaia comes Ouranos, the Sky, made just as big as she is, so that he can cover her on every side.

17
00:01:04,513 --> 00:01:07,573
Gaia and Ouranos have children.

18
00:01:07,773 --> 00:01:10,161
Twelve of them are Titans.

19
00:01:10,361 --> 00:01:14,738
Three are Cyclopes, one-eyed makers of the thunderbolt.

20
00:01:14,938 --> 00:01:20,839
And three are Hundred-Handers, giants with a hundred arms and fifty heads each.

21
00:01:21,239 --> 00:01:25,380
Ouranos hates the three Hundred-Handers most of all.

22
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:30,349
And he hides his children away inside Gaia, as each one is born.

23
00:01:30,749 --> 00:01:33,976
Gaia groans, cramped with her own children.

24
00:01:34,176 --> 00:01:40,578
So she makes a sickle out of grey flint, and asks her sons to use it on their father.

25
00:01:40,978 --> 00:01:43,539
They are all too scared to answer.

26
00:01:43,739 --> 00:01:45,104
All but one.

27
00:01:45,304 --> 00:01:48,366
Cronos, the youngest, the clever one.

28
00:01:48,766 --> 00:01:53,483
Gaia hides Cronos in ambush, and puts the sickle in his hand.

29
00:01:53,683 --> 00:01:57,083
When Ouranos comes at night, Cronos strikes.

30
00:01:57,283 --> 00:02:00,296
And Cronos takes his father's place.

31
00:02:00,696 --> 00:02:03,836
Ouranos has one thing left to say.

32
00:02:04,036 --> 00:02:07,652
He calls his sons Titans, as an insult.

33
00:02:07,852 --> 00:02:17,645
Hesiod says the word comes from a verb meaning to strain, because, Ouranos says, they strained too far, and did a terrible thing.

34
00:02:18,045 --> 00:02:21,939
And he promises that one day, someone will pay for it.

35
00:02:22,489 --> 00:02:23,923
Remember that.

36
00:02:25,723 --> 00:02:27,959
Cronos is king now.

37
00:02:28,159 --> 00:02:32,312
And he has learned a prophecy from Gaia and Ouranos.

38
00:02:32,512 --> 00:02:35,698
One day, his own son will overcome him.

39
00:02:36,098 --> 00:02:38,606
So Cronos makes a rule.

40
00:02:38,806 --> 00:02:44,224
Every child that his wife Rhea bears, he swallows as it is born.

41
00:02:44,624 --> 00:02:46,108
Five children.

42
00:02:46,458 --> 00:02:47,633
Hestia.

43
00:02:47,733 --> 00:02:48,962
Demeter.

44
00:02:49,062 --> 00:02:50,059
Hera.

45
00:02:50,159 --> 00:02:51,208
Hades.

46
00:02:51,308 --> 00:02:52,508
Poseidon.

47
00:02:52,608 --> 00:02:55,056
Rhea's grief never ends.

48
00:02:55,256 --> 00:03:03,046
When she is about to bear a sixth, she goes to her own parents, Gaia and Ouranos, and asks for a plan.

49
00:03:03,446 --> 00:03:05,234
They send her to Crete.

50
00:03:05,434 --> 00:03:10,553
In a cave on a mountain, Gaia takes the baby from her, and hides him.

51
00:03:10,953 --> 00:03:16,047
To Cronos, Gaia gives a great stone, wrapped in baby clothes.

52
00:03:16,247 --> 00:03:17,500
He takes it.

53
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:19,134
He swallows it.

54
00:03:19,334 --> 00:03:21,365
And he never notices.

55
00:03:21,715 --> 00:03:23,738
That baby is Zeus.

56
00:03:23,938 --> 00:03:25,207
The youngest.

57
00:03:25,407 --> 00:03:27,736
The one who was not eaten.

58
00:03:28,136 --> 00:03:30,043
Hesiod skips ahead.

59
00:03:30,243 --> 00:03:32,182
The boy grows strong.

60
00:03:32,382 --> 00:03:36,105
Gaia tricks Cronos, and Zeus overpowers him.

61
00:03:36,305 --> 00:03:40,521
That is all the poem says about how the gods got free.

62
00:03:40,921 --> 00:03:43,655
Cronos throws them all back up.

63
00:03:43,855 --> 00:03:47,670
The stone comes first, because it was swallowed last.

64
00:03:48,070 --> 00:03:57,656
Zeus plants that stone in the ground at Pytho, another name for Delphi, below Mount Parnassus, so that people will see it and wonder.

65
00:03:58,056 --> 00:04:04,266
More than eight hundred years later, a Greek traveller named Pausanias visits Delphi.

66
00:04:04,466 --> 00:04:07,093
And he finds a small stone.

67
00:04:07,493 --> 00:04:11,909
Every day, he writes, the locals pour olive oil over it.

68
00:04:12,109 --> 00:04:15,220
At every feast, they lay raw wool on it.

69
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:21,535
And some say this is the stone that was given to Cronos in place of his child.

70
00:04:21,935 --> 00:04:27,002
Zeus also frees his uncles, the Cyclopes, who have been chained up.

71
00:04:27,202 --> 00:04:31,329
They thank him with thunder, lightning, and the thunderbolt.

72
00:04:31,529 --> 00:04:35,042
Until then, Gaia had hidden the thunder away.

73
00:04:35,842 --> 00:04:37,254
Now the war.

74
00:04:37,454 --> 00:04:40,446
The Titans hold Mount Othrys.

75
00:04:40,646 --> 00:04:44,008
Zeus and the young gods hold Olympus.

76
00:04:44,408 --> 00:04:47,342
Hesiod does not say what started it.

77
00:04:47,542 --> 00:04:52,636
He only says they fight for ten full years, and neither side can win.

78
00:04:53,036 --> 00:04:55,198
Then Gaia speaks again.

79
00:04:55,398 --> 00:05:01,134
She tells the gods that with the three Hundred-Handers on their side, they will win.

80
00:05:01,334 --> 00:05:06,878
But the Hundred-Handers are still chained under the earth, where Ouranos put them.

81
00:05:07,278 --> 00:05:10,890
So Zeus and the gods bring them up to the light.

82
00:05:11,090 --> 00:05:14,194
Then they give them everything they need.

83
00:05:14,394 --> 00:05:18,100
Nectar and ambrosia, the food of the gods.

84
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:20,431
Their spirit comes back.

85
00:05:20,631 --> 00:05:25,921
Zeus reminds them how he freed them, and asks them to show their strength.

86
00:05:26,121 --> 00:05:29,883
Cottus, one of the three, answers for all of them.

87
00:05:30,083 --> 00:05:31,423
They will fight.

88
00:05:31,823 --> 00:05:33,204
The sea rings.

89
00:05:33,404 --> 00:05:35,162
The earth crashes.

90
00:05:35,362 --> 00:05:43,782
Olympus shakes on its foundations, and the shock reaches all the way down to Tartarus, the pit beneath the world.

91
00:05:44,182 --> 00:05:46,945
And now Zeus stops holding back.

92
00:05:47,145 --> 00:05:50,637
He comes down from Olympus, hurling lightning.

93
00:05:50,837 --> 00:05:52,659
The forests crackle.

94
00:05:52,859 --> 00:05:54,897
The sea starts to boil.

95
00:05:55,097 --> 00:05:58,782
The heat, Hesiod says, seizes Chaos itself.

96
00:05:59,182 --> 00:06:04,447
The Hundred-Handers throw three hundred rocks, one on top of another.

97
00:06:04,647 --> 00:06:08,638
They bury the Titans beneath the earth, and bind them.

98
00:06:09,038 --> 00:06:12,528
Ouranos had promised that someone would pay.

99
00:06:12,728 --> 00:06:15,346
Now the Titans are paying.

100
00:06:15,746 --> 00:06:17,917
How deep is Tartarus?

101
00:06:18,117 --> 00:06:20,315
Hesiod gives a measurement.

102
00:06:20,515 --> 00:06:28,285
A bronze anvil dropped from the sky takes nine days to reach the earth, and nine more to reach Tartarus.

103
00:06:28,985 --> 00:06:32,500
Poseidon fits gates of bronze to the prison.

104
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:38,809
And in one passage, Hesiod has the three Hundred-Handers stay on as its guards.

105
00:06:39,209 --> 00:06:44,074
In Hesiod's telling, the freed prisoners are the key to the victory.

106
00:06:44,274 --> 00:06:48,134
For ten years, the young gods could not win alone.

107
00:06:48,334 --> 00:06:51,175
Ouranos had chained these three.

108
00:06:51,375 --> 00:06:56,055
Zeus and the gods freed them, fed them, and asked for their help.

109
00:06:56,855 --> 00:06:59,467
The Titans are locked away.

110
00:06:59,667 --> 00:07:02,100
But the poem is not finished.

111
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:05,802
Gaia bears one more child, her youngest.

112
00:07:06,002 --> 00:07:07,306
Typhoeus.

113
00:07:07,506 --> 00:07:10,942
His father is Tartarus, the pit itself.

114
00:07:11,342 --> 00:07:19,674
Hesiod gives him a hundred snake heads, with dark, flickering tongues, and fire flashing from the eyes of every one.

115
00:07:20,074 --> 00:07:22,536
And a voice in every head.

116
00:07:22,736 --> 00:07:27,098
Sometimes it makes a sound the gods can understand.

117
00:07:27,298 --> 00:07:36,409
Sometimes it bellows like a bull, or roars like a lion, or yelps like puppies, or hisses until the mountains ring.

118
00:07:36,809 --> 00:07:41,000
That day, Hesiod says, something past help would have happened.

119
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:47,659
Typhoeus would have ruled over gods and mortals, if Zeus had not noticed him in time.

120
00:07:48,059 --> 00:07:49,658
Zeus thunders.

121
00:07:49,858 --> 00:07:51,462
Olympus shakes.

122
00:07:51,662 --> 00:07:57,403
Hades trembles in the underworld, and the Titans tremble in Tartarus.

123
00:07:57,603 --> 00:08:02,768
Then Zeus leaps down from Olympus and burns every one of those heads.

124
00:08:03,168 --> 00:08:08,287
Typhoeus crashes down, burned and broken, and the earth groans.

125
00:08:08,487 --> 00:08:12,460
The ground itself melts, Hesiod says, like tin.

126
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:15,968
And Zeus throws him into Tartarus.

127
00:08:16,368 --> 00:08:22,499
From Typhoeus come the storm winds, the ones that scatter ships and wreck sailors.

128
00:08:22,699 --> 00:08:28,863
Only three winds are not his: the north wind, the south wind, and the west wind.

129
00:08:29,663 --> 00:08:31,496
Now look at this jar.

130
00:08:32,296 --> 00:08:43,903
It is a water jar, a hydria, painted around five hundred and forty B.C. That is roughly a century and a half after Hesiod, by the usual dates.

131
00:08:44,103 --> 00:08:46,914
It sits in a museum in Munich.

132
00:08:47,314 --> 00:08:51,630
On the left, Zeus, with a thunderbolt raised in his hand.

133
00:08:51,830 --> 00:08:54,584
His name is painted beside him.

134
00:08:54,984 --> 00:08:57,189
On the right, the monster.

135
00:08:57,389 --> 00:09:03,229
The painter gave him a human face, huge wings, and a body of coiling snakes.

136
00:09:03,629 --> 00:09:08,587
Hesiod gave him a hundred snake heads, and never mentions wings.

137
00:09:08,987 --> 00:09:13,691
Maybe a painter simply has the curve of a pot to work with.

138
00:09:14,491 --> 00:09:18,075
Now the war is over, and the monster is gone.

139
00:09:18,475 --> 00:09:22,665
After the war, the gods urge Zeus to rule over them.

140
00:09:22,865 --> 00:09:25,726
And Gaia is the one who prompts them.

141
00:09:26,126 --> 00:09:30,292
Cronos took his father's place with a sickle in his hand.

142
00:09:30,492 --> 00:09:32,286
Zeus is urged.

143
00:09:32,686 --> 00:09:35,769
He divides the honors among the gods.

144
00:09:35,969 --> 00:09:37,935
Each one gets a share.

145
00:09:38,135 --> 00:09:39,872
And then he marries.

146
00:09:40,272 --> 00:09:42,804
His first wife is Metis.

147
00:09:43,004 --> 00:09:47,429
Hesiod says she is wiser than all gods and all mortals.

148
00:09:47,829 --> 00:09:51,240
Metis is about to give birth to Athena.

149
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:57,620
That is when Zeus deceives her with cunning words, and puts her inside his own belly.

150
00:09:58,020 --> 00:10:02,083
He does it on the advice of Gaia and Ouranos.

151
00:10:02,283 --> 00:10:09,244
And the reason is stated plainly: so that no one else will ever take Zeus's place as king.

152
00:10:09,644 --> 00:10:13,442
Because Metis is destined to have two children.

153
00:10:13,642 --> 00:10:17,904
First, a daughter, as strong as her father, and as wise.

154
00:10:18,104 --> 00:10:24,019
And after her, a son with an overbearing spirit, who would be king of gods and men.

155
00:10:24,419 --> 00:10:27,606
Zeus does not wait for the son to grow up.

156
00:10:27,806 --> 00:10:30,805
He does not wait for the son to be born.

157
00:10:31,005 --> 00:10:32,942
He swallows the mother.

158
00:10:33,342 --> 00:10:36,270
Ouranos chained some of his children.

159
00:10:36,470 --> 00:10:38,561
Cronos swallowed his.

160
00:10:38,761 --> 00:10:42,546
Zeus swallowed the mother, before the son could exist.

161
00:10:42,946 --> 00:10:45,780
And notice who gives the advice.

162
00:10:46,030 --> 00:10:47,867
Gaia made the sickle.

163
00:10:48,117 --> 00:10:50,023
Gaia hid the baby.

164
00:10:50,273 --> 00:10:52,109
Gaia tricked Cronos.

165
00:10:52,359 --> 00:10:55,566
Gaia told the gods how to win the war.

166
00:10:55,816 --> 00:10:59,028
Gaia prompted them to make Zeus king.

167
00:10:59,278 --> 00:11:06,047
And Gaia, with Ouranos, tells Zeus how to make sure that nobody ever takes his place.

168
00:11:06,447 --> 00:11:11,814
Again and again in this poem, Gaia is behind the turns of power.

169
00:11:12,614 --> 00:11:15,220
Three rulers of the sky.

170
00:11:15,420 --> 00:11:18,477
The first was struck down by his son.

171
00:11:18,677 --> 00:11:21,869
The second was overthrown by his.

172
00:11:22,069 --> 00:11:27,334
The third made sure that the child who could do it to him was never born.

173
00:11:27,734 --> 00:11:30,448
Hesiod passes no judgment.

174
00:11:30,648 --> 00:11:35,333
He calls it a deception, done with cunning words, and moves on.

175
00:11:35,533 --> 00:11:38,670
And right after, Zeus marries again.

176
00:11:39,070 --> 00:11:41,432
But the poem leaves a puzzle.

177
00:11:41,632 --> 00:11:45,272
Metis was swallowed before Athena was born.

178
00:11:45,472 --> 00:11:53,036
And yet, a little further on, Hesiod says that Zeus himself gave birth to Athena, from his own head.

179
00:11:53,436 --> 00:12:00,030
Next time: the goddess who came out of her father's head, and what Hesiod says about her mother.
